// PORT-PENDING 1103 tests-jit-no-unwind-boundary: tests/jit_no_unwind_boundary.rs:1-947
// Translate in place: replace the //RS lines below with the 1:1 Jet port (keep `// Rust: <file>:<line>` anchors).
//RS //! D-JITUNWIND1 (#1995 / #1997): the mechanical check behind the JIT's
//RS //! no-unwind boundary, plus the cross-tier regression it protects.
//RS //!
//RS //! The invariant: **no Rust panic may be raised while a Cranelift frame is on
//RS //! the stack, and no `extern "C"` frame `crates/jet-jit` exposes to generated
//RS //! code may be reached by an unwind.** `cranelift-jit` registers no unwind
//RS //! information for the code it emits, so a panic above a JIT frame cannot be
//RS //! walked past — libgcc's phase 1 returns `_URC_END_OF_STACK` and the process
//RS //! rtaborts with `fatal runtime error: failed to initiate panic, error 5`
//RS //! before any outer `catch_unwind` can see it. A bare `SIGABRT` with no text is
//RS //! not one of Jet's exit codes.
//RS //!
//RS //! The chosen mechanism is boundary conversion, and its recorded cost is that
//RS //! the guarantee has to hold across ~1.7k host symbols — "so it needs a
//RS //! mechanical check, not review discipline." This file is that check. The
//RS //! decision record is `crates/jet-jit/src/host_seam.rs` and
//RS //! `Docs/spec/architecture.md` R13.
//RS //!
//RS //! Why the checks below and not "does each seam catch?": the guarantee is
//RS //! structural, not per-body.
//RS //!
//RS //! 1. A host seam is a plain Rust `fn`. rustc gives an `extern "C"` **body** an
//RS //!    abort-on-unwind shim, so a panic inside one dies at that body's own edge
//RS //!    as `thread caused non-unwinding panic` — a *different* abort from error
//RS //!    5, and one no wrapper above it could ever catch. So a boundary can only
//RS //!    be added by replacing the C frame. **Any** `extern` fn defined in this
//RS //!    crate is therefore an unguardable seam by construction, whatever its ABI
//RS //!    and whatever it is called; the one exception is stated positively, as the
//RS //!    shim that *is* the boundary. The first form of this check banned
//RS //!    `extern "C" fn jet_*` only, and `Ffi.rs`'s `jit_ffi_reporter` — the frame
//RS //!    a foreign library enters when a foreign function has already failed, with
//RS //!    a Cranelift frame below it — sat outside that family for as long as the
//RS //!    family was the rule. There used to be a second exception, the OS signal
//RS //!    handler, because this crate compiled its own copy of it; #2027 deleted
//RS //!    that copy, so the handler is now checked where it actually lives (see
//RS //!    `SIGNAL_HANDLER_OWNER`) and a copy reappearing here is a bug on both
//RS //!    counts.
//RS //! 2. The only `extern "C"` frames generated code can reach are the shims
//RS //!    `host_seam::guarded` builds, and the only ways to reach one are the
//RS //!    single `builder.symbol` call inside `host_fns!` and `guarded_addr`. Any
//RS //!    other route from a host function to an address is an escape.
//RS //! 3. The generator must still generate the guard. Without this, reverting one
//RS //!    line of `host_fns!` would silently unguard every symbol and leave every
//RS //!    other test green — and the same is true of the shim's own body, which is
//RS //!    why check 1's `host_seam.rs` exemption is pinned rather than assumed.
//RS //! 4. An abort may never become a *ledger row*. The structural checks above
//RS //!    cover the frames this crate compiles; the example corpus is where an
//RS //!    escape actually shows up, and every ratcheted section of
//RS //!    `tests/jit_corpus_gate.txt` is shrink-only, so a filed abort is an abort
//RS //!    under permanent protection. `corpus_gate_refuse_abort`
//RS //!    (`tests/dev_parts/support.rs`) refuses to classify one on any tier, using
//RS //!    the `ABORT_MARKERS` list below so the two checks cannot drift.
//RS //!
//RS //! Run: `Tools/agent/jet-env cargo test --test jit_no_unwind_boundary`
//RS 
//RS mod common;
//RS 
